{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 64, :comments ["<b>But among these there was not a man.</b> But there were women — for the decree of the spies was decreed only upon the men, because the men hated the land and said, ״נִתְּנָה רֹאשׁ וְנָשׁוּבָה מִצְרָיְמָה״ (Numbers 14:4), whereas the women cherished the land, as the daughters of Zelophehad said, ״תְּנָה לָּנוּ אֲחֻזָּה״. And I offer two reasons for the matter: for there is a land that raises the modest, and there is a land that raises adulterers, and our Sages of blessed memory said (Numbers Rabbah 20:22) that Shittim raised adulterers, and therefore they committed harlotry at Shittim, for dwelling in that place caused them to commit harlotry; but the land of Israel has the innate property that the land does not tolerate lewdness, as is written in the passage of forbidden relations (Leviticus 18:25–27): ״כִּי אֶת כָּל הַתּוֹעֵבוֹת עָשׂוּ אַנְשֵׁי הָאָרֶץ״, ״וַתָּקִיא הָאָרֶץ אֶת יוֹשְׁבֶיהָ״. And we have already written above that the men were given to sexual immorality and therefore hated the land, whereas the women were virtuous and modest — and Shelomit bat Dibri testifies to this, for Scripture publicized her; therefore they cherished the land that raises the modest. And know that this is so, for even Shittim, which raises adulterers, would draw its healing from the holy land, as it says (Joel 4:18): ״וּמַעְיָן יֵצֵא מִבֵּית ה׳ וְהִשְׁקָה אֶת נַחַל הַשִּׁטִּים״. And for this reason it was written ״וּבְאֵלֶּה לֹא הָיָה אִישׁ״ specifically at the counting, to tell you: but there were women — for in this counting the modesty of the women is alluded to more than that of the men, inasmuch as the heh was moved before the yod, as explained above." "A second reason is this: for it is well known that there is a distinction between the land of Israel and other lands, for in the land of Israel rains are more prevalent than in the land of Egypt or other lands. Now in the land of Egypt, since rains are not prevalent there, those who work the soil must water the grain fields with their feet, just as one waters a vegetable garden; and since they have such great toil in the fields, the Torah exempted them from terumot and ma'asarot, for the line of justice does not require that one give a share to one who did not labor in it. But the land of Israel, where rains fall in their season and the owner of the field does not have such great toil in watering the fields — and even though he has the toil of sowing and plowing and other labors of the field — nevertheless the Holy One, blessed be He, also has a share in the field, inasmuch as He, blessed be He, gives rain in its season; therefore it is just that they give a share from the portion of the Most High to the priests and Levites, the ministers of our Elohim, for from the table of the Most High they receive their due." "And this matter is explicitly explained in the passage of Ekev (Deuteronomy 11:10–12), where it says: ״כִּי הָאָרֶץ אֲשֶׁר אַתָּה בָא שָׁמָּה לְרִשְׁתָּהּ לֹא כְאֶרֶץ מִצְרַיִם הִיא אֲשֶׁר יְצָאתֶם מִשָּׁם אֲשֶׁר תִּזְרַע אֶת זַרְעֲךָ וְהִשְׁקִיתָ בְרַגְלְךָ כְּגַן הַיָּרָק, וְהָאָרֶץ אֲשֶׁר אַתָּה בָא שָׁמָּה לְרִשְׁתָּהּ לִמְטַר הַשָּׁמַיִם תִּשְׁתֶּה מָּיִם, אֶרֶץ אֲשֶׁר עֵינֵי ה׳ אֱלֹהֶיךָ דּוֹרֵשׁ אוֹתָהּ״ and so forth. And the Yalkut on the passage of Ekev (§842) concludes that specifically that [land] was given for 'derishah' — to separate from it challah, terumah, and ma'asarot — and not other lands, etc. And anyone who has eyes to see and a heart to understand will marvel at the sight: what is the relevance of challah and terumah here? And what does it mean that 'it' (אוֹתָהּ) is to the exclusion of other lands from terumah and ma'aser, and why was this exclusion written here? Rather, it is certain that He wished to give a reason why the land of Israel is obligated in challah, terumah, and ma'asarot more than other lands, and He said: because in other lands all the toil is yours — for even a field of grain you must water with your foot like a vegetable garden — therefore the Torah exempted you from terumot and ma'asarot. But the land that you are coming there to inherit — ״לִמְטַר הַשָּׁמַיִם תִּשְׁתֶּה מָּיִם״, and if so, half the labor is yours and half is the Most High's; therefore it is just that you give the portion of the Most High to the ministers of our Elohim." "And for this reason the men hated the land, for they were stout-hearted men, far removed from charity — they had no desire or will to go from a place of exemption to a place of obligation. And with a full mouth they said thus: ״זָכַרְנוּ אֶת הַדָּגָה אֲשֶׁר נֹאכַל בְּמִצְרַיִם חִנָּם״ (Numbers 11:5), and they interpreted in the Sifrei: חִנָּם — free from the commandments. And the explanation of the matter is that they ate without terumot and ma'asarot, for on this account they counted there the cucumbers and the melons and so forth, because all these are exempt from tithes outside the land even by rabbinic decree, whereas in the land of Israel they are obligated by rabbinic decree, because all these are a vegetable garden, and require watering by foot even in the land of Israel, for they require an abundance of water. And the language of Scripture proves this, for it says: ״וְהִשְׁקִיתָ בְרַגְלְךָ כְּגַן הַיָּרָק״ — hear from this that a vegetable garden, without qualification, everywhere requires watering. Therefore even in the land of Israel they are exempt by Torah law, and the Sages obligated them since there is an abundance of water there; but not outside the land, where there is no abundance of water. And from the fact that they said חִנָּם מִן הַמִּצְווֹת — hear from this that their eye was stingy toward the priests of HaShem; therefore they hated the land, the place of obligation." "But the women of that generation were righteous women and loved charity — both the commandment of challah, which is uniquely assigned to women and dependent on the land, and the rest of terumot and ma'asarot. Therefore they loved the land, to go to a place of obligation, for an ordinary woman has no authority or control over her husband's property; therefore they were eager to go to a place where the obligation is imposed upon their husbands to give. And this is what our Sages of blessed memory said (Sotah 11b): in the merit of the righteous women who were in that generation, our ancestors were redeemed from Egypt — that is to say, in their merit they went out from a place of exemption to a place of obligation, which their soul longed for and yearned toward. And so our Sages of blessed memory said (Bava Batra 119b) of the daughters of Zelophehad, that they were righteous women — that is, because they loved charity, therefore they said ״תְּנָה לָּנוּ אֲחֻזָּה״. And a sound and good proof for our words is what Rashi explained on ״לְמִשְׁפְּחוֹת מְנַשֶּׁה בֶן יוֹסֵף״ — why did he mention Joseph? Because he cherished the land, etc., and so did his daughters. And you already know that these two good traits were in Joseph: one, that he was guarded from sexual immorality — ״וַיֵּשֶׁב בְּאֵיתָן קַשְׁתּוֹ״ (Genesis 49:24) — with the wife of Potiphar; and according to the second reason, that they loved the land on account of charity, Joseph was a lover of righteousness, for he sustained and provided for his entire father's house, and his daughters inherited these two traits of his, and on their account they said ״תְּנָה לָּנוּ אֲחֻזָּה״. And this is what they said, that they were righteous women, in that they married only those fitting for them — so the Gemara concludes. And the explanation of the matter, that they married into the tribe of Joseph specifically, is that they too held the deeds of their fathers in their hands, to be lovers of righteousness, and they were wise women in that they were guarded from sexual immorality — the opposite of what is said (Numbers 5:12) ״כִּי תִשְׂטֶה אִשְׁתּוֹ״, the language of folly (שְׁטוּת). And this is a precious interpretation."], :book :numbers, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 26, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:43:45.014884528Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}